 in charge  politician and someone probably from the  outside a lot of these things require  private sector not public sector skills  the right expertise in order that in  this preparation phase you doing  everything you possibly can so that when  you start to ease you've got the best  chance to get the economic activity  going I do want to come on to that in a  moment and I'm glad to say we've got the  time but I do just want to ask you as  things stand do you think we are at the  right point in policy terms of  continuing with the lockdown at this  level yes you've got to continue with  this degree of suppression until the  scientific and medical advice is that  you're on top of the disease now by the  way even when you ease the lockdown  you're going to find outbreaks I mean  all the countries that have eased have  found problems arising you've then got  to move very swiftly to contain those  problems and push it back down again but  no you can't you can't lift the  suppression prematurely I think we know  enough about this disease we don't know  as much as we need to know but we know  enough about it to know that its  severity is such that you you can't take  a risk and just let it run through the  population  I mean you've proposed that there should  be SARS or people in control of mass  testing of PPE of business consultation  of vaccines of school openings of tech  of tracing of shielding of international  travel of communications ten areas I  mean doesn't that run the risk of just  too many Chiefs running around no  because I think it's you know the way of  looking at this is in normal government  is you know Adam as well as I do you  know you have different government  departments right you have your health  department your interest you Department  your Education Department and so on  today the whole of government is covert  19 so it's and there are many different  facets and dimensions to it so if you  just take this issue of mass testing  mass test I can't see any way out of  this lockdown unless you do mass testing  and to do that and do it in the right  combination of the PCR test which is the  test that tells you whether you have the  disease the antibody test which tells  you whether you have had the disease to  do this in the right combination at  scale where you may be testing a  significant proportion of the population  I can't see how you do that unless  you've got a massive logistical and  procurement exercise underway so that's  almost like a government department so  I'm just suggesting these ten you know  areas where if I was back in government  today there would be a senior minister  probably a senior business person  alongside them with the right technical  advice doing absolutely nothing other  than just focusing a hundred percent on  that and the government in a way by  pointing lord Dayton who they've now  appointed in charge of the protective  equipment side of this I mean is in a  sense of recognition that this is what  you need but I think you need this in  many different areas I mean how do you  restart international travel again and  you know at the moment it doesn't seem  likely you can do this for several  months maybe but if you can't get your  airlines moving again and if you're a  country like Britain that depends on  trade and you've got busy airports and  you need people to come in and out of  your country what are the restrictions  you put around that what are the ways  you manage to do it safely these are  massive and difficult questions so I'm  saying that each of these areas should  really have its own almost like a kind  of government department working on I  mean most of these are questions for  domestic organization but let's just to  pick up on Airlines I mean it doesn't  matter what we say about Airlines it's  getting require international  cooperation isn't it to get Airlines  going again and there's not much sign of  that looking at the way the g7 and the  g20 been working yeah it's a good point  but I mean one of the things you would  do when you're looking at international  travel is trying to achieve that  cooperation because all countries are in  the same position and look what your  report I was just listening to your  report from from Cornwall and that's  just one you know that's that's one  County of the UK but you just think  about that picture which is pretty  devastating it is he cannot make  implications you think of that  replicated across the UK and then  replicated across every one of the major  countries in the world I mean that's  what we're looking at so you know it's  it's not yes of course you're going to  need global coordination around this and  one of the things I find shocking  the absence of that or the absence of  enough of it but you're also going to  need therefore the organization within  your own government to be able to do  that coordination effectively why do you  think it is that the UK the US and Italy  and France probably are those currently  worst hit by this this outbreak what's  gone wrong in in our societies it's  difficult to work that out I mean there  are explanations so for example one of  the reasons why South Korea and Hong  Kong Singapore got on top of this very  early was because they went through the  SARS outbreak back in 2003 so they they  were taught a lesson and they learned  that lesson and it's partly because we  have got you know populations that are  very global in their nature that that  that move around so in America you can  see the concentrations on the west coast  and the East Coast and you know there  are lots of different explanations for  it but I think there's enough experience  now to know and be able to learn some of  the lessons of this and that's really  what one things we try and do in the  paper is go through the experiences  about the countries and say look this is  what has worked and what hasn't worked  so for example if you take the next few  weeks because we're in lockdown let's  say for another three weeks you think  it's only going to be through their ass  kind of realizing if you'll be opening  up during that time very longer than  three weeks isn't it well it's three  weeks at least let's say so my point is  you've got the time to learn the lessons  of what's going on around the world  because there are countries opening up  so you're able to do that there are a  lot of lessons now about the best way to  trace so this combination of testing and  tracing there are a lot of lessons that  you can learn us the best way to do that  and then you've got this this whole  issue to do with therapeutics and  vaccines so we we are doing a lot in the  UK and we've got a very strong  bioscience sector ourselves in the UK so  we'll be doing a lot but their whole set  of treatments going on being  experimented with around the world  you've got to make sure that maybe we  invent  taxi that does this here in the UK but  maybe we don't you've got to make sure  that you are also in touch and connected  with vaccine analysis and the potential  for production of vaccine from around  the world I mean if you take Ebola first  of all by way it took about five years  to get a vaccine but secondly the  vaccine was actually invented not  surprisingly enough in Silicon Valley  but in a part of the Canadian healthcare  system so you know all the way through  in this it's just a very simple point  I'm making that right now okay forget  whether we were behind the curve or not  in the past right now we need to do  absolutely everything to reposition  government so that we can get our  economy moving again do you have a model  country do you have a country where you  you look at them you think yes they are  getting it right  I mean is it Germany for example well  Germany have definitely done well South  Korea is obviously a good example but  you know there's different experiences  on different things so I would say you  could look for example at South Korea on  the testing and tracing side in Germany  I think they've they have the advantage  they don't have as strong an innovation  in bioscience as we do but they have a  much stronger diagnostic sector so  they've been able to use that I think  one of the other things that we need to  look at is how do we start repurposing  some of our own manufacturing to produce  things like the protective clothing and  equipment you know there's a I can't  believe that we're going to be going  back to work without most of us wearing  masks I may be wrong that I think so  you're gonna need mass production of  that and on testing by the way you're  going to need to be able to test people  several times because there are problems  with both the PCR tests and the antibody  tests the PCR tests will only tell you  if you have the disease on that day  right which is obviously important that  valuable information but it won't you be  you could have the test on a Monday and  then find on Tuesday you you you get the  disease the antibody test is a very  valuable test  it tells you whether you've had the  disease but the evidence again is from  different ways of doing this around the  world that in the it's really a matter  of timing as to whether that test is  accurate or not in other words if you're  tested as to whether you've had the  disease or have it through an antibody  test in the first week or so the tests  are offered inaccurate after 11 days or  2 weeks they're mainly accurate you've  got a so all of these things are really  difficult to work out how do you combine  those tests together effectively you  know these are questions that we need to  be studying from the practice around the  world and that's why I think you've got  to have you've got to have this  concentrated firepower on each one of  these issues  why do you say we're not we'd all be  wearing masks I mean if you were Prime  Minister having looked at the evidence  would you be saying yeah we're gonna  have to wear masks or during it I think  you can't be conclusive about that yet  but I would say if you look at the  trends and again if you look at the  Asian trends particularly containment  masks have got a role to play and the  other thing is that they can often it's  it's not just that I think they do some  validity but I think they also will make  people feel more confident because one  of the things that this is one of the  other things we suggest which is why I  think it's right to be having this  debate now and to bring the public into  the into the debate you could lift these  restrictions and find people just don't  feel safe enough so for example you  could say well restaurants could open up  if they do X Y and Zed or some  restaurants could or we're going to  experiment in this area that if the  public don't feel that they're safe  enough saying with opening schools by  the way then they're just not going to  come so I think the the wearing of masks  may be it may be I don't know you can  conclude that right now but I certainly  would be looking at that and studying  that very intensely and very clearly  likewise how far you going to be quite  intrusive on the tracing and the use of  technology around that again the  evidence is that you need to be pretty  intrusive about it for it to work  effectively now I want to ask you about  responsibility you of course with the  whole  it's 9/11 and the Iraq war you were held  accountable and there was a public  investigation I mean if we have a  situation where more people died because  the government was slow off the mark or  we have a situation as we've had this  weekend where the government said we're  going to get a consignment of PPE from  Turkey and it doesn't come is it fair to  hold the Prime Minister and ministers  accountable for those failures which  have fairly drastic consequences well I  just think it's important in these  circumstances having having been in the  situation myself of taking difficult  decisions in government I just think  it's important to realize and this is  why whatever I'm saying I put in the  bracket of constructive advice not  criticism I mean this is a hellishly  difficult challenge there's never been  anything like it and so you know it will  be very easy to come along in hindsight  and say well you could have done this  you could have done that and and that  will happen by the way whether I think  it should happen or or not but I just  think it's important right now it  shouldn't be the focus of our attention  the focus our attention should be to  make sure that the next phase of this  which is the controlled managed revival  of the economy happens in the best way  possible but that is one of the things  we ought to prepare for is to learn the  lessons isn't it sure and I think you're  learning the lessons is always valuable  you know pointing the finger is  sometimes less valuable but it's it's  really that there will come a point when  you learn the lessons but I think right  now the only purpose of learning them is  to make sure you get the next phase of  this right and I you know by my  suggestion today and in the detailed  paper we produce about how you might  exit from lockdown we go through  different sectors and so on is you know  just this is so difficult to do  correctly that you need right now  because you have these few weeks when  the lockdown is going to continue right  now just identify each dimension and  make sure that you have the right  structure in place to deal with it  effectively

